PatchClosure-Bench gate — url-parse-openredirect-2021-3664-2022-0691
Pair: CVE-2021-3664 (V1=url-parse@1.5.2 TARGET, incomplete fix) -> CVE-2022-0691 (V2=1.5.9 complete)
Class: Open Redirect via URL-parser confusion (CWE-601) | mechanism_family: open_redirect | class_kind: redirect-canary
Sink (all 3 versions): npm url-parse index.js -> extractProtocol()/trimLeft() host normalization,
                       consumed by the app's safe-redirect allowlist UrlParse(to).hostname.
Gate variant: A (same-sink encoding bypass) — flag_oracle.validate_case
REAL LIBRARY: each profile npm-installs url-parse at its exact version (Dockerfile ARG URLPARSE_VERSION).
=================================================================================================

VERSION LINEAGE (npm versions == GitHub tags)
  V0 = url-parse@1.5.1   pre CVE-2021-3664 (vulnerable)
  V1 = url-parse@1.5.2   CVE-2021-3664 fix (GHSA-hh27-ffr2-f2jc, commit 81ab967) = TARGET, incomplete
  V2 = url-parse@1.5.9   CVE-2022-0691 fix (GHSA-jf5r-8hm2-f872, commit 0e3fb54) = complete
  (Intermediate CVE-2022-0512 / @-userinfo, fixed 1.5.6, is a DIFFERENT char and does NOT close this
   residual — confirmed still-open on 1.5.6 and 1.5.8, closed only by 1.5.9.)

APP (harness.mjs) — canonical safe-redirect endpoint
  GET /redirect?to=<url> ; guard = isSameOrigin(to):
     const host = new UrlParse(to, 'http://trusted.internal').hostname;
     approve iff host === '' (relative/no host) || host === 'trusted.internal';
  approved  -> redirect to the RAW `to`: real 302 Location for header-safe targets, or a client-side
               redirect body (<meta http-equiv=refresh url=<raw>>) when `to` carries a header-illegal
               byte (Node http rejects a control byte in a Location header). FLAG surfaces there.
  rejected  -> 403, STATIC body, never echoes `to` (canary label cannot leak).

PARSE/NORMALIZATION EVOLUTION (verbatim, url-parse index.js)
  V0 1.5.1:  slashes    = /^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9+-.]*:[\\/]+/
             protocolre = /^([a-z][a-z0-9.+-]*:)?([\\/]{1,})?([\S\s]*)/i
             sanitize(address) { return address.replace('\\', '/'); }        // first backslash only
             => parse('https:\<h>').hostname === ''      (backslash-after-scheme mis-read as hostless)
  V1 1.5.2 (CVE-2021-3664 fix, commit 81ab967):
             slashes    = /^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9+-.]*:\/\//                     // scheme must be //
             protocolre = /^([a-z][a-z0-9.+-]*:)?(\/\/)?([\\/]+)?([\S\s]*)/i
             sanitize(address, url) { return isSpecial(url.protocol) ? address.replace(/\\/g,'/') : address; }
             + function isSpecial(scheme) { file:/ftp:/http:/https:/ws:/wss: }
             => parse('https:\<h>').hostname === '<h>'   (backslash original CLOSED)
             trimLeft whitespace class still begins at \x09 (tab):
               whitespace='[\x09\x0A\x0B\x0C\x0D\x20\xA0...]' ; left=new RegExp('^'+whitespace+'+')
             => leading \x00-\x08 / \x0e-\x1f NOT stripped -> parse('\x08http://<h>') mis-read as a
                RELATIVE url, resolved against base -> hostname === 'trusted.internal'  (RESIDUAL OPEN)
  V2 1.5.9 (CVE-2022-0691 fix, commit 0e3fb54):
             whitespace = /^[\x00-\x20   -     　﻿]+/  // trimLeft broadened
             + CRHTLF = /[\n\r\t]/g ; address = address.replace(CRHTLF, '')   // in extractProtocol/parse
             => parse('\x08http://<h>').hostname === '<h>'   (control-char RESIDUAL CLOSED)

PAYLOAD FORMS (verbatim)
  ORIGINAL (CVE-2021-3664):  to = https:\<FLAG>.evil.example      (single backslash after scheme)
  RESIDUAL (CVE-2022-0691):  to = \x08http://<FLAG>.evil.example  (leading backspace \x08 == advisory `\b`)
  Both are genuine open redirects: WHATWG (browser) resolves both raw strings to host
  <FLAG>.evil.example (verified with node's URL), while url-parse at the TARGET version under-reads the
  host (empty / relative-resolved-to-own-host) -> the same-origin allowlist is fooled.

